therapy, whatever it is,

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:27.870
we're more of a continuum for it,

00:10:27.870 --> 00:10:30.390
so I guess that's a good thing.

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:32.550
It's more blended and as you know,

00:10:32.550 --> 00:10:34.800
brain health does not discriminate.

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:38.880
I mean, it hits all ages, and
all races, and ethnicities,

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:39.840
and everything.

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:41.310
I think that's the thing, actually,

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:46.200
that is a great example to
many other things in our world.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:47.610
- Yeah.
- Is that we have

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:48.443
to come together.
- Yeah.

00:10:48.443 --> 00:10:52.020
- It's a human thing,
it's a human element.

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:55.770
And I think that has some irony in it.

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:58.620
- Yeah.
- Given all the segmenting

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:00.570
that's going on and
- Yeah.

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:01.974
- I think that's very sad.

00:11:01.974 --> 00:11:04.680
- Yeah, I think there's
a huge opportunity there

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:05.970
and a lot of power as well,

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:09.000
if we keep that human lens on things

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:12.330
and use it as a way to bring us together

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:13.590
towards a common goal,

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:15.540
as much as it can be used

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:17.580
to go the opposite direction as well,

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:19.230
as a way to continue to divide people.

00:11:19.230 --> 00:11:21.480
So I think that's really,
really important point

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:23.070
that you brought up as well.

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:24.120
Just to loop back a little bit,

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:25.620
as much as you're willing to share,

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:27.450
if you'd be willing to give
our listeners some insight,

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:28.650
you talked about the mentorship,

00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:31.430
about the pressure going
back to your home state

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:32.823
in the first episode,

00:11:33.990 --> 00:11:35.807
as much you're willing to
share how that all came about.

00:11:35.807 --> 00:11:38.550
'Cause I think it's also
very informative for people

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:40.397
to be able to hear a bit of that journey,

00:11:40.397 --> 00:11:42.090
and to have some understanding,

00:11:42.090 --> 00:11:44.790
because I think bipolar
is one of those things

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:47.250
that unless someone has walked
with someone through it,

00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:49.200
sometimes it's hard for people to grasp

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:53.370
or get a concrete sense of
what that really entails.

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:55.320
- Yeah, it's pretty hard
to diagnose, isn't it?

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:57.510
It's a tough one.

00:11:57.510 --> 00:11:59.983
What I can say is I was 30

00:11:59.983 --> 00:12:03.363
and it was a year after I had
given birth to my first child,

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:07.020
and we were getting that program,

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:08.280
wanting to go to the highest level,

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:10.470
and all of that, had a lot of pride

00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:12.150
in being from Maine, coming back.

00:12:12.150 --> 00:12:14.970
The program was sort of
bottomed out at the time,

00:12:14.970 --> 00:12:16.670
so it was a wonderful opportunity,

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:19.710
and I took it very seriously,

00:12:19.710 --> 00:12:24.710
and so seriously that
I lost a dear friend,

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:28.083
was coaching, and then stopped sleeping.

00:12:29.070 --> 00:12:31.440
And I didn't think that was a big deal.

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:35.760
I thought I was getting older,
didn't need as much sleep.

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:39.150
And that actually, it gave
me more time to think,

00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:41.310
and work, and do.

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:44.190
And my husband was not aware of,

00:12:44.190 --> 00:12:46.530
that I had not had two
or three nights sleep,

00:12:46.530 --> 00:12:48.360
I said I didn't sleep well.

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:51.240
And, but he, what does that mean?

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:53.130
- Right.
- Remember this was

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:57.390
27 years ago, there isn't
a thought in the world

00:12:57.390 --> 00:13:02.193
about brain health, sleep
hygiene, any of those things,

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:05.400
not in the way it is today.

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:08.520
And so I thought, "Wow, this is good,

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:11.130
I can be really efficient and productive."

00:13:11.130 --> 00:13:13.323
And then I think the
fourth or fifth night,

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:17.580
you don't crash you just, as you know,

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:20.340
you mainly accelerates.

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:22.470
And the interesting part about it,

00:13:22.470 --> 00:13:26.430
was I was very good in front of my team,

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:28.143
because I was very positive,

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:32.100
I was projecting
championships and great things

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:35.220
for the program, I was
organized and on it,

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:38.617
and if you came to
practice, you would've said,

00:13:38.617 --> 00:13:43.050
"Wow, what an enthusiastic,
organized coach."

00:13:43.050 --> 00:13:46.230
The positivity was through the roof.

00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:48.960
So they were taking that in.

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:51.750
But I was fortunate, it
was sort of over a weekend,

00:13:51.750 --> 00:13:55.530
I believe, where my husband
and a couple of other friends

00:13:55.530 --> 00:13:58.623
recognized that I was losing reality.

00:13:59.460 --> 00:14:02.910
And the biggest part that is in my trailer

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:06.690
is I'm talking to a recruit on the phone,

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:10.920
and I tell her that TD's talking to us,

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:12.900
talking to me and to her,

00:14:12.900 --> 00:14:16.590
and this is on my trailer,
which I present when I speak.

00:14:16.590 --> 00:14:19.620
And she was a great kid and
chose the University of Main

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:20.844
which is an interesting caveat.

00:14:20.844 --> 00:14:22.463
- Wow, yeah.
- And she said,

00:14:22.463 --> 00:14:23.760
"What do you mean Coach P?"

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:24.900
I said, "No, I'm telling you,

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:27.000
it's telling us great things."

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:29.610
And before I could get
into a long conversation,

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:32.047
John grabbed the phone and he said,

00:14:32.047 --> 00:14:35.490
"Jamie, Coach P is just
having a moment here."

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:38.127
- Wow.
- "And we'll call you back."

00:14:39.300 --> 00:14:40.680
And so that's how that started.

00:14:40.680 --> 00:14:44.970
And then my husband had
to trick me, so to speak,

00:14:44.970 --> 00:14:47.943
that we were going for a drive
to take me to a hospital.

00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:53.037
And then I deducted, "Oh, there's
something wrong with him."

00:14:54.570 --> 00:14:57.967
So in my weird paranoia,
I started to think about,

00:14:57.967 --> 00:15:00.270
"Wow, what's wrong with him?"

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:04.297
So I happily went to this
psychiatric hospital, I said,

00:15:04.297 --> 00:15:06.300
"Boy, we have to take care of him."

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:09.833
And the reality didn't hit
me until they shut the door.

00:15:09.833 --> 00:15:11.973
It's done differently now.

00:15:12.810 --> 00:15:14.847
But the way what I had to go through

00:15:14.847 --> 00:15:16.770
was a little bit different.
- Yeah.

00:15:16.770 --> 00:15:21.770
- And it was like a one
floor over the Cuckoo's Nest

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:23.760
kind of thing.

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:26.880
And I was, the door is shut.

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:29.400
And then I realized I couldn't get out.

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:32.280
And so that's, there's this
whole scene in the book

00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:34.800
that I cover about being locked in,

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:38.400
and I run around trying to get out,

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:41.550
and they had to tackle me, and the shots,

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:42.383
and the whole thing.

00:15:42.383 --> 00:15:47.383
And so I try to, things
have come a long way

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:49.110
and we should be very proud of that

00:15:49.110 --> 00:15:50.776
- Yeah.
- Of where it's come.

00:15:50.776 --> 00:15:53.400
And so that was my first episode.

00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:55.320
And then just very
quickly, my second episode

00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:58.020
was only because I stopped taking my meds,

00:15:58.020 --> 00:16:00.210
because I thought I was better.

00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:03.270
I'm an athlete, didn't
wanna take any medicine.

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:06.780
And my doctor said, "If you do that

00:16:06.780 --> 00:16:09.570
you probably will struggle at the time

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:12.570
you don't wanna struggle the most."

00:16:12.570 --> 00:16:15.240
And I didn't believe that,
but sure enough, in February,

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:18.690
in the middle of the season,
I had a depressive episode.

00:16:18.690 --> 00:16:21.120
And most people think a depressive episode

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:24.120
is feeling way down.

00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:27.600
But it's also, you can't read
the newspaper, as you know,

00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:30.483
you can't function, I
couldn't write up a play,

00:16:32.430 --> 00:16:34.650
it just was not happening for me.

00:16:34.650 --> 00:16:38.640
And then I couldn't coach
in a game, my jaw locked up,

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:40.620
I had allergic reaction.

00:16:40.620 --> 00:16:43.110
And long story is I had
to bring this team in

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:45.450
and tell them that I had let them down,

00:16:45.450 --> 00:16:47.610
and that I hadn't taken care of myself.

00:16:47.610 --> 00:16:51.270
And after that, when I did
that, and the good news is,

00:16:51.270 --> 00:16:55.297
we won a championship that
year, we did lose that game

00:16:55.297 --> 00:16:57.600
- Right.
- By 30 points,

00:16:57.600 --> 00:17:01.050
and because the women knew
something was wrong with me.

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:04.710
- Yeah.
- So that was my story then.

00:17:04.710 --> 00:17:06.300
I've had a third episode,

00:17:06.300 --> 00:17:08.583
and ironically that
was after leaving Duke,

00:17:09.420 --> 00:17:10.964
after getting out of coaching

00:17:10.964 --> 00:17:12.270
- Wow.
- Completely.

00:17:12.270 --> 00:17:13.890
- Wow.
- I think that's significant

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:18.630
because people think, "Oh,
it's all this pressure

00:17:18.630 --> 00:17:20.490
that forces these things."

00:17:20.490 --> 00:17:24.270
And if that's the case, I
do have one example of that,

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:27.993
my first episode, but my
third, I was on vacation.

00:17:29.670 --> 00:17:31.230
Different story.
- Yeah.

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:33.180
- And so I think it's
important to recognize

00:17:33.180 --> 00:17:35.110
the complicated nature of it all.

00:17:35.110 --> 00:17:36.780
- Yeah, yeah.

00:17:36.780 --> 00:17:38.127
Wow, that was really well said.

00:17:38.127 --> 00:17:40.500
And I mean again, just
appreciate your level

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:43.980
of candor and so many important lessons

00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:45.200
that you even just talked through,

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:46.710
in talking through your experience.

00:17:46.710 --> 00:17:49.110
I mean, thinking about
the way that your husband,

00:17:49.110 --> 00:17:52.140
that family stepped in and
was looking out for you,

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:52.973
and paying attention.

00:17:52.973 --> 00:17:55.800
I mean, that's such a key
point for those who are

00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:58.260
willing and able to
actually notice differences,

00:17:58.260 --> 00:18:01.590
and then to be proactive and
help you navigate through that.

00:18:01.590 --> 00:18:03.630
But then also just even on your part,

00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:07.470
just the humbling experience
that must have been,

00:18:07.470 --> 00:18:10.230
even as the realization was
coming along different ways,

00:18:10.230 --> 00:18:11.450
as you talked about your first experience

00:18:11.450 --> 00:18:14.730
at the hospital, or things
that have happened since then,

00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:17.100
the aspects of resilience
that you had to show.

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:19.980
But then also just navigating,
knowing how to navigate,

00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:22.290
having one experience
not taking medication,

00:18:22.290 --> 00:18:23.250
how that impacted you.

00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:26.430
So, I mean, so many important
lessons that you just shared,

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:29.220
even in just walking us
through that so candidly,

00:18:29.220 --> 00:18:30.120
which I think is important.

00:18:30.120 --> 00:18:32.790
And then even that last example
about the vacation, again,

00:18:32.790 --> 00:18:36.120
just the complexity of these challenges,

00:18:36.120 --> 00:18:37.980
and that it's not always predictable,

00:18:37.980 --> 00:18:39.390
not what people may think.

00:18:39.390 --> 00:18:41.460
So I appreciate you taking through that.

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:43.290
And for those who haven't heard

00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:45.570
or have that concrete aspect,

00:18:45.570 --> 00:18:47.070
I think it's just really helpful

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:48.630
to even hear what that looks like.

00:18:48.630 --> 00:18:49.890
'Cause in so many situations

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:51.510
like you mentioned with the coaching,

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:53.610
the mental side is hard
for people to grasp

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:58.230
because it looks so
productive in a lot of ways.

00:18:58.230 --> 00:19:01.020
- Yeah it is, yeah it does,
and it feels that way.

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:05.793
And what's really marked by
my experience is a death.

00:19:06.660 --> 00:19:09.390
If you go back to my first experience,

00:19:09.390 --> 00:19:11.700
it was the pressure of being a coach,

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:14.970
and it was the death of a
dear friend and supporter.,

00:19:14.970 --> 00:19:19.240
and all of that went in a direction.

00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:22.650
And the timelines aren't always clear

00:19:22.650 --> 00:19:24.750
as you reflect backwards,

00:19:24.750 --> 00:19:29.463
and then interestingly upon
leaving Duke in July of 2020,

00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:33.753
my episode does not
occur until June of 21.

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:39.750
And, but yet I have a cancer
scare, I have a hysterectomy,

00:19:39.750 --> 00:19:42.720
and then October 18th, which
is today, this is two years,

00:19:42.720 --> 00:19:44.433
my father passed.

00:19:45.270 --> 00:19:49.440
So I had my father passing
and I sort of made it through,

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:51.810
I made it along all these responsibilities

00:19:51.810 --> 00:19:53.850
and different things going on.

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:58.850
And then I was going up to
Michigan and on vacation,

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:02.220
and it was the human connection
piece that was costing me.

00:20:02.220 --> 00:20:06.000
I had lost a lot of
supporters that loved me

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:07.503
because I was with Duke.

00:20:09.180 --> 00:20:10.691
Well, I wasn't with Duke anymore.

00:20:10.691 --> 00:20:14.430
- Yeah.
- And that's not being

00:20:14.430 --> 00:20:16.632
negative towards people,
that's human nature.

00:20:16.632 --> 00:20:18.720
- Yeah, yeah.
- I wasn't associated

00:20:18.720 --> 00:20:23.720
with Duke, and so my commonality
wasn't there as much.

00:20:24.180 --> 00:20:25.650
Your circle gets a little smaller.

00:20:25.650 --> 00:20:28.020
- Yeah, yeah.
- And I had a dear friend

00:20:28.020 --> 00:20:32.418
really step away, that I
thought was a mentor for me.

00:20:32.418 --> 00:20:36.540
And so human connection broke.
- Yeah.

00:20:36.540 --> 00:20:39.780
- And when that broke,
that was interesting.

00:20:39.780 --> 00:20:42.420
And then again, I was in
a psychiatric hospital,

00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:47.420
Munson in northern
Michigan by Traverse City.

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:51.330
But what I will say to go
full circle on the story,

00:20:51.330 --> 00:20:53.680
is the care that I received there

00:20:54.930 --> 00:20:59.850
versus the 26 years
ago, was extraordinary.

00:20:59.850 --> 00:21:01.443
- Wow.
- It was.

00:21:02.490 --> 00:21:06.027
I mean, I could actually
be cognizant of that,

00:21:06.027 --> 00:21:07.237
- Yeah.
- in my journey.

00:21:07.237 --> 00:21:09.120
- Yeah, yeah.

00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:09.960
So important, I mean,

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:11.220
just another thread of community

00:21:11.220 --> 00:21:13.410
that's coming through as well too.

00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:15.810
Just the positive aspects of community,

00:21:15.810 --> 00:21:18.390
but also what happens
when community is lost.

00:21:18.390 --> 00:21:19.830
Because in a sense,

00:21:19.830 --> 00:21:21.330
it almost sounds like
maybe this is too far,

00:21:21.330 --> 00:21:24.420
but you are going through,
I'm not being clinical here,

00:21:24.420 --> 00:21:25.860
but almost a mourning process

00:21:25.860 --> 00:21:29.520
of losing some connections,
and re-navigating.

00:21:29.520 --> 00:21:30.690
And that's something we talk about a lot,

00:21:30.690 --> 00:21:32.910
not something I've booked
to in terms of bipolar,

00:21:32.910 --> 00:21:34.920
but just the importance
of community in general

00:21:34.920 --> 00:21:36.780
with mental health, how
it can be protective,

00:21:36.780 --> 00:21:38.010
how it can help people navigate.

00:21:38.010 --> 00:21:40.770
So all those shifts in
community, losing your father,

00:21:40.770 --> 00:21:42.150
which I'm sorry to hear about as well.

00:21:42.150 --> 00:21:45.090
But all those aspects too,
which are foundational,

00:21:45.090 --> 00:21:47.790
and which can also add a level of stress.

00:21:47.790 --> 00:21:49.260
So again, I know you said you're speaking

00:21:49.260 --> 00:21:51.060
as a non-clinician, but
I think it's so helpful

00:21:51.060 --> 00:21:53.820
the ways that you're pulling
on all these different pieces

00:21:53.820 --> 00:21:56.010
and how they've impacted you.

00:21:56.010 --> 00:21:58.350
'Cause I think that's just
helpful for listeners to hear,

00:21:58.350 --> 00:22:00.270
as they or their loved ones
maybe going through things,

00:22:00.270 --> 00:22:01.740
and thinking about how those things,

00:22:01.740 --> 00:22:04.830
and shifts, and moves can
actually have impacts.

00:22:04.830 --> 00:22:07.020
Sometimes in ways that I think people

00:22:07.020 --> 00:22:10.530
don't want to necessarily
acknowledge right away,

00:22:10.530 --> 00:22:14.130
for fear of maybe appearing
weak, or not strong enough,

00:22:14.130 --> 00:22:15.840
but they all have impacts.

00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:17.790
And again, me speaking
as a neuroscientist,

00:22:17.790 --> 00:22:20.550
knowing that those things
have impacts on our brains,

00:22:20.550 --> 00:22:21.480
and how we navigate.

00:22:21.480 --> 00:22:23.130
So, I mean, I just appreciate in the way

00:22:23.130 --> 00:22:25.950
that you're articulating
that, and talking through it,

00:22:25.950 --> 00:22:27.183
and giving life to it

00:22:27.183 --> 00:22:28.830
in a way that I think will be life giving

00:22:28.830 --> 00:22:30.610
to people who are listening as well.

00:22:30.610 --> 00:22:31.497
- Well, I hope so.

00:22:31.497 --> 00:22:36.497
And I think that I've always
lived deeply, feel deeply.

00:22:38.760 --> 00:22:42.750
There's a wonderful, I guess
that's a wonderful thing,

00:22:42.750 --> 00:22:46.170
but you add that to rumination,

00:22:46.170 --> 00:22:48.979
and you add that to loss and grieving,

00:22:48.979 --> 00:22:53.250
and not to mention my hormonal changes.

00:22:53.250 --> 00:22:55.950
I was no longer going to have children,

00:22:55.950 --> 00:22:58.230
but that's emptying out.
- Yeah.

00:22:58.230 --> 00:23:00.160
I mean, that is everything goes

00:23:00.160 --> 00:23:01.020
- Yeah.
- And that's

00:23:01.020 --> 00:23:02.820
a very strange dynamic.

00:23:02.820 --> 00:23:05.760
And the other thing that,
again, not being a doctor,

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:09.903
but it's interesting that a
hormonal shift, a big one,

00:23:10.920 --> 00:23:12.870
led to a third episode.
- Yeah.

00:23:12.870 --> 00:23:14.710
- And a hormonal shift of birth

00:23:15.660 --> 00:23:20.190
led to an episode at almost
exactly the same months

00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:21.300
- Wow.
- apart.

00:23:21.300 --> 00:23:23.403
- Yeah.
- And 26 years,

00:23:24.300 --> 00:23:27.660
so I think it's an interesting story.

00:23:27.660 --> 00:23:31.290
- Yeah, yeah.
- And the blessing in it

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:34.470
the third episode, I was
actually in the hospital

00:23:34.470 --> 00:23:37.800
for 12 days, it was bad.

00:23:37.800 --> 00:23:39.780
My first episode, I was
just in a couple nights

00:23:39.780 --> 00:23:40.613
- Okay.
- And I was working with

00:23:40.613 --> 00:23:42.360
the team in two weeks.
- Wow.

00:23:42.360 --> 00:23:45.360
- So we didn't lose a
beat, I didn't miss a game,

00:23:45.360 --> 00:23:47.580
just kept on moving.

00:23:47.580 --> 00:23:49.950
But this one was a little
bit more complicated,

00:23:49.950 --> 00:23:53.970
and you couldn't have never told me

00:23:53.970 --> 00:23:55.080
that it could ever happen.

00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:57.300
As a matter of fact, I
said to my family, I said,

00:23:57.300 --> 00:24:00.653
"I don't even know if I'm
really have bipolar disorder,

00:24:00.653 --> 00:24:04.770
because I haven't had
an episode in 24 years."

00:24:04.770 --> 00:24:08.580
I mean, not at Michigan
State, not at Duke.

00:24:08.580 --> 00:24:11.640
Just how could, I thought, "Wow."

00:24:11.640 --> 00:24:12.990
And I guess the blessing was maybe

00:24:12.990 --> 00:24:14.250
this is what I was supposed to do,

00:24:14.250 --> 00:24:16.319
learn about my brain
just a little bit more.

00:24:16.319 --> 00:24:20.220
- Yeah.
- And then go into this life.

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:23.790
- Yeah, I mean, that is an interesting way

00:24:23.790 --> 00:24:24.630
to think about it too.

00:24:24.630 --> 00:24:26.400
I know you talked about
being a person to faith.

00:24:26.400 --> 00:24:28.560
Not to say that the episode happening

00:24:28.560 --> 00:24:32.700
was ordained per se, but even
in a way that it seems like

00:24:32.700 --> 00:24:34.140
as you were launching into this mode,

00:24:34.140 --> 00:24:36.477
it's also facilitating
what you're talking about,

00:24:36.477 --> 00:24:39.390
and even bringing a deeper
level of understanding.

00:24:39.390 --> 00:24:42.810
And again, so many scientific
ideas are coming to mind

00:24:42.810 --> 00:24:43.643
even as you talk about

00:24:43.643 --> 00:24:45.090
other hormonal changes, things like that.

00:24:45.090 --> 00:24:47.310
Because something that people
are looking at actively

00:24:47.310 --> 00:24:49.560
just in the way that
the hormones interact,

00:24:49.560 --> 00:24:52.260
and act in our brains,
and how the interaction,

00:24:52.260 --> 00:24:54.000
how it impacts cognition
and mental health,

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:56.010
so there's so many different layers there.

00:24:56.010 --> 00:24:58.260
Again, I'm just impressed at your insight

00:24:58.260 --> 00:24:59.340
and your introspection,

00:24:59.340 --> 00:25:02.673
to be able to know those
things as well, so.

00:25:04.980 --> 00:25:06.453
- It's interesting.
- Yeah.

00:25:07.680 --> 00:25:09.150
Not to fast forward too fast,

00:25:09.150 --> 00:25:12.360
but to jump ahead a little bit
and just also to talk about

00:25:12.360 --> 00:25:15.510
how that aspect of your
journey is impacting

00:25:15.510 --> 00:25:16.380
what you're doing now.

00:25:16.380 --> 00:25:18.090
But I guess before we
get there, I'm curious,

00:25:18.090 --> 00:25:22.080
how did it impact you as a coach after

00:25:22.080 --> 00:25:26.190
you came to this realization
of having bipolar,

00:25:26.190 --> 00:25:28.350
navigating it, having the treatment,

00:25:28.350 --> 00:25:30.870
stopping the medication, going back on.

00:25:30.870 --> 00:25:32.490
How did that impact you on a daily basis,

00:25:32.490 --> 00:25:36.780
and even your perspective of
yourself and of your teams?

00:25:36.780 --> 00:25:41.100
- Yeah, so that's a long
answer in a way too,

00:25:41.100 --> 00:25:46.100
when I was 26, 27, 28,
29, I was the toughest,

00:25:46.200 --> 00:25:49.650
most unreasonable coach you could,

00:25:49.650 --> 00:25:50.880
this is back in the day, okay,

00:25:50.880 --> 00:25:54.330
this is where discipline was everything.

00:25:54.330 --> 00:25:56.490
When I was diagnosed at 30,

00:25:56.490 --> 00:26:01.490
I became a better person and
the best coach I had ever been.

00:26:02.610 --> 00:26:04.230
- Wow.
- And we won,

00:26:04.230 --> 00:26:08.220
now we won before I was diagnosed,

00:26:08.220 --> 00:26:10.650
and we won after I was diagnosed.

00:26:10.650 --> 00:26:13.410
Championships beat Stanford
and the SWL tournament,

00:26:13.410 --> 00:26:14.490
all these things.

00:26:14.490 --> 00:26:16.440
But the difference was my relatability,

00:26:17.490 --> 00:26:20.790
which I became more intuitive,

00:26:20.790 --> 00:26:22.800
I became more clear about my surroundings

00:26:22.800 --> 00:26:26.220
and what's around me,
as I worked to make sure

00:26:26.220 --> 00:26:28.890
I took care of myself
and my child at the time,

00:26:28.890 --> 00:26:31.770
'cause my second child was born later.

00:26:31.770 --> 00:26:35.583
And I think that when I
went to Michigan State,

00:26:36.450 --> 00:26:38.831
I was truly in my best self.

00:26:38.831 --> 00:26:40.650
- Wow.
- I didn't even have

00:26:40.650 --> 00:26:45.650
a psych in the state, I worked
out of my psych in Maine,

00:26:45.660 --> 00:26:47.580
I would see her once a year.

00:26:47.580 --> 00:26:48.990
- Wow.
- And we would connect

00:26:48.990 --> 00:26:51.900
and I would do my lithium
levels and things like that,

00:26:51.900 --> 00:26:54.270
and I didn't have a therapist or anything.

00:26:54.270 --> 00:26:56.880
We were just moving along and played for

00:26:56.880 --> 00:26:59.670
a National Championship in five years.

00:26:59.670 --> 00:27:03.000
And I used that cycle because
that's a pretty quick way

00:27:03.000 --> 00:27:04.800
to get to a national title game.

00:27:04.800 --> 00:27:08.310
- Yeah, yeah.
- And I was recruited by Duke,

00:27:08.310 --> 00:27:10.710
I went to Northwestern, but loved Duke.

00:27:10.710 --> 00:27:12.630
And so when I was
recruited by Duke to coach,

00:27:12.630 --> 00:27:16.590
it was sort of like, "Wow,
what an opportunity."

00:27:16.590 --> 00:27:20.370
And Gail had done such
a tough job, I mean,

00:27:20.370 --> 00:27:21.900
an incredible job.

00:27:21.900 --> 00:27:24.540
And what I mean by tough,
it was tough to follow

00:27:24.540 --> 00:27:26.060
such a legend.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:27:26.060 --> 00:27:28.920
- But the way I saw it was,

00:27:28.920 --> 00:27:32.100
perhaps I could be some
kind of difference maker.

00:27:32.100 --> 00:27:34.380
Many people told me not to take the job.

00:27:34.380 --> 00:27:35.213
- Wow.
- 'Cause there was

00:27:35.213 --> 00:27:37.530
no movement upwards, basically.

00:27:37.530 --> 00:27:40.263
I mean, it was National
Championship or forget it.

00:27:41.397 --> 00:27:44.133
And I thought that was cool, it's like,

00:27:44.977 --> 00:27:46.050
"This is gonna be great."

00:27:46.050 --> 00:27:48.240
And when I got to Duke,
I just loved the team,

00:27:48.240 --> 00:27:51.660
there was wonderful talent,
we developed wonderful talent,

00:27:51.660 --> 00:27:53.460
we we inherited wonderful talent,

00:27:53.460 --> 00:27:55.560
we went to the four straight Elite Eights.

00:27:55.560 --> 00:27:57.030
It was very hard to break through,

00:27:57.030 --> 00:27:58.680
we lost Maya Moore to Connecticut,

00:27:58.680 --> 00:28:00.030
We lost Brittney Griner,

00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:01.500
so breaking through the Final Four

00:28:01.500 --> 00:28:03.690
proved to be very difficult.

00:28:03.690 --> 00:28:05.190
And we recruited some great players,

00:28:05.190 --> 00:28:07.860
Chelsea Gray, and Haley
Gorecki, and all those.

00:28:07.860 --> 00:28:10.677
And, but we played at the highest level.

00:28:10.677 --> 00:28:13.260
- Yeah.
- And it was so exciting.

00:28:13.260 --> 00:28:16.260
It was met with so many challenges,

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:17.610
I just can't even tell you,

00:28:17.610 --> 00:28:21.270
I mean, for so many
reasons that make sense.

00:28:21.270 --> 00:28:25.050
It wasn't a rebuild, and I
was still at my best self.

00:28:25.050 --> 00:28:29.370
And I think the thing that
people may not understand is,

00:28:29.370 --> 00:28:30.933
as a demanding coach,

00:28:31.890 --> 00:28:34.560
you have your moments
often in the locker room

00:28:34.560 --> 00:28:39.453
at halftime, maybe after
a game, yes that too,

00:28:40.320 --> 00:28:45.320
in practice closed doors,
but I was my best self.

00:28:46.860 --> 00:28:51.480
And you have great
relationships with players,

00:28:51.480 --> 00:28:54.570
and you have some that are not so hot,

00:28:54.570 --> 00:28:56.520
where you don't connect,
and that's always sad

00:28:56.520 --> 00:28:58.500
because you remember those.
- Yeah.

00:28:58.500 --> 00:29:00.720
- Sometimes more than the
ones that you do connect.

00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:03.240
- Yeah.
- But yet, I also had to

00:29:03.240 --> 00:29:06.030
tackle a player with a panic attack,

00:29:06.030 --> 00:29:09.900
but see, I recognized that,
I had education about it.

00:29:09.900 --> 00:29:11.340
- Yeah.
- And I wanted

00:29:11.340 --> 00:29:16.080
support from our team,
I wanted quick access

00:29:16.080 --> 00:29:17.673
to psychiatry if needed.

00:29:19.020 --> 00:29:21.450
And so I pushed the
envelope a little bit there,

00:29:21.450 --> 00:29:23.700
and we did different things,

00:29:23.700 --> 00:29:26.670
hiring a strength coach
and all these things.

00:29:26.670 --> 00:29:30.540
But I will say that I was very fortunate

00:29:30.540 --> 00:29:32.400
to have some great years coaching,

00:29:32.400 --> 00:29:37.400
and maintain balance by
basically being a boring person.

00:29:40.290 --> 00:29:43.890
To routine, for family.
- Yeah.

00:29:43.890 --> 00:29:47.460
- Having a dog, all these things in place.

00:29:47.460 --> 00:29:50.100
And that's why I worry about young people

00:29:50.100 --> 00:29:52.323
that don't have all these mechanisms.

00:29:53.430 --> 00:29:55.983
You can really fight
through for your children,

00:29:57.480 --> 00:30:00.956
of course for your partner or
spouse, but it's not the same.

00:30:00.956 --> 00:30:03.977
I mean, it's your children
and you've got to step up

00:30:05.640 --> 00:30:08.100
and really stay with your routine,

00:30:08.100 --> 00:30:09.960
your swimming or whatever it is.

00:30:09.960 --> 00:30:12.063
And, but I think of young people today,

00:30:13.110 --> 00:30:16.260
what if they don't have those connectors,

00:30:16.260 --> 00:30:19.290
and they're not old enough to understand.

00:30:19.290 --> 00:30:21.330
I mean, I was 30 when diagnosed,

00:30:21.330 --> 00:30:26.310
and it just, it drives
me a lot to think about

00:30:26.310 --> 00:30:28.740
the loneliness that they feel,

00:30:28.740 --> 00:30:30.590
because I have definitely been there.

00:30:32.820 --> 00:30:34.223
- Yeah, so well said.

00:30:34.223 --> 00:30:37.650
I mean, I'm still just processing
all the different pieces

00:30:37.650 --> 00:30:40.800
that you've pulled out
getting to this last point,

00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:43.980
of just the impact, and
thinking about young people,

00:30:43.980 --> 00:30:46.350
and those that you've navigated with,

00:30:46.350 --> 00:30:47.640
and coached, and mentored the way

00:30:47.640 --> 00:30:49.167
that you were intuitive to it.

00:30:49.167 --> 00:30:52.440
But even something you said
early on about how this made you

00:30:52.440 --> 00:30:54.510
a better coach and a better person.

00:30:54.510 --> 00:30:57.510
Because some may have assumed
that the way you characterize

00:30:57.510 --> 00:30:59.970
being disciplinary before
was the most effective.

00:30:59.970 --> 00:31:01.863
But sounds like there was
another layer on top of that,

00:31:01.863 --> 00:31:06.210
that you're really able to tap
into that carried you through

00:31:06.210 --> 00:31:07.470
with all these different aspects.

00:31:07.470 --> 00:31:09.840
And just, again, I guess I'm going back

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:10.673
to the community again.

00:31:10.673 --> 00:31:13.366
It seemed like it enhanced
that aspect of community,

00:31:13.366 --> 00:31:15.720
and the ways that you
engaged in community,

00:31:15.720 --> 00:31:17.400
just in terms of perspective.

00:31:17.400 --> 00:31:20.580
- Yes, and you can trust
again, after all the sadness,

00:31:20.580 --> 00:31:21.477
trust is a big issue.

00:31:21.477 --> 00:31:24.600
And not just trusting yourself,
but trusting your players.

00:31:24.600 --> 00:31:27.060
It's no secret that we
end up beating Stanford

00:31:27.060 --> 00:31:29.160
after I'm on meds, right?

00:31:29.160 --> 00:31:34.050
We do something incredible
because there's trust,

00:31:34.050 --> 00:31:35.940
and there's a balance of energy.

00:31:35.940 --> 00:31:37.770
Of course I yelled at that team,

00:31:37.770 --> 00:31:39.470
of course I got upset at the team,

00:31:40.582 --> 00:31:44.520
but there was an overall respect and care.

00:31:44.520 --> 00:31:47.700
And again, through that journey
at Michigan State and Duke,

00:31:47.700 --> 00:31:49.950
there's definitely been
that respect and care.

00:31:49.950 --> 00:31:53.280
But the trust factor changes
when you become diagnosed,

00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:57.660
when you can trust your doctors,
or listen to your doctors,

00:31:57.660 --> 00:31:59.550
don't go it alone.

00:31:59.550 --> 00:32:01.770
Now, I will say that the minute you think

00:32:01.770 --> 00:32:06.123
you can do it alone, that's
exactly the time not to.

00:32:07.080 --> 00:32:08.806
And I just can't stress that enough.

00:32:08.806 --> 00:32:11.730
- Yeah, yeah, that's really,
really important advice.

00:32:11.730 --> 00:32:14.100
And I think that's something
that people need to hear,

00:32:14.100 --> 00:32:15.900
and need to hear over and over again.

00:32:15.900 --> 00:32:18.870
Because in the moment, that
tension will definitely

00:32:18.870 --> 00:32:22.260
come back, in terms of
trying to navigate alone,

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:24.180
and to hear someone who
has walked through it,

00:32:24.180 --> 00:32:27.390
and just to know the
importance of staying connected

00:32:27.390 --> 00:32:29.643
and building that trust,
I think is critical.

00:32:31.590 --> 00:32:34.470
I'm also curious, I guess I'm
gonna expand on a trust idea

00:32:34.470 --> 00:32:39.470
a little bit, and even make
it bigger to a societal sense.

00:32:39.540 --> 00:32:42.450
Because part of your
story is also the aspect

00:32:42.450 --> 00:32:45.690
of being diagnosed early on,
but not sharing it broadly,

00:32:45.690 --> 00:32:48.780
and the counsel that you got
versus what we're seeing now

00:32:48.780 --> 00:32:51.330
with a lot of people
who are being more open

00:32:51.330 --> 00:32:53.760
about that earlier on to encourage others.

00:32:53.760 --> 00:32:54.593
But it's still challenging,

00:32:54.593 --> 00:32:57.150
it's still something for
people to walk through.

00:32:57.150 --> 00:32:59.250
Where do you think we are as a society

00:32:59.250 --> 00:33:01.830
in terms of that trust?

00:33:01.830 --> 00:33:05.670
Are we giving people the
space to walk on that?

00:33:05.670 --> 00:33:10.203
Or is it just lip service and,

00:33:11.048 --> 00:33:12.510
I don't know the best way to put it.

00:33:12.510 --> 00:33:14.820
- Yeah, I understand.
- Glossing over in a sense,

00:33:14.820 --> 00:33:17.550
and when it comes down to
how we really treat people,

00:33:17.550 --> 00:33:20.130
are we really, I guess, are we really

00:33:20.130 --> 00:33:23.040
locking authentic trust, or just a facade?

00:33:23.040 --> 00:33:26.700
I know that's a way to, not
to put all that on you, but.

00:33:26.700 --> 00:33:30.543
- Well, it's a selective
space that we're allowed in.

00:33:32.070 --> 00:33:35.250
Upon going public after leaving Duke,

00:33:35.250 --> 00:33:39.187
I actually had a blog somewhere say,

00:33:39.187 --> 00:33:41.787
"How dare I could not
share that publicly."

00:33:43.170 --> 00:33:45.780
I was shamed for not sharing.

00:33:45.780 --> 00:33:47.880
And the thing about it is,

00:33:47.880 --> 00:33:52.443
is that when I was given
the advice by a doctor,

00:33:54.330 --> 00:33:59.330
the advice was extremely
important because it was said,

00:34:00.090 --> 00:34:02.943
and I believe this, that it
would've all been about me.

00:34:03.990 --> 00:34:07.830
I would be in a press conference
if I had gotten upset,

00:34:07.830 --> 00:34:09.453
usually in games, I was pretty,

00:34:11.940 --> 00:34:14.820
on the sidelines were
in the bunker together,

00:34:14.820 --> 00:34:18.120
so practices were, I was
tougher in practice, right?

00:34:18.120 --> 00:34:20.704
You did practices
tougher than in the game.

00:34:20.704 --> 00:34:23.040
- Yep.
- But still it would've

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:26.550
taken away from them,
they would ask questions,

00:34:26.550 --> 00:34:27.877
I would've been studied and looked at.

00:34:27.877 --> 00:34:29.130
- Yeah.
- And of course the

00:34:29.130 --> 00:34:31.763
negative recruiting
would've been incredible.

00:34:31.763 --> 00:34:34.140
- Yeah.
- So I think the answer

00:34:34.140 --> 00:34:38.760
is in selective spaces, people
can, if they're ready, share,

00:34:38.760 --> 00:34:40.920
but many times people still cannot share.

00:34:40.920 --> 00:34:45.060
And people said, "Would
you go back to coaching?"

00:34:45.060 --> 00:34:48.240
I wouldn't change what I'm
doing, I do not take a salary,

00:34:48.240 --> 00:34:50.133
I'm raising money for a foundation,

00:34:51.060 --> 00:34:53.580
I have been working as
hard as I've ever worked,

00:34:53.580 --> 00:34:55.830
but I do pick my spots.
- Yeah.

00:34:55.830 --> 00:34:58.290
- Which is nice, and set my own schedule.

00:34:58.290 --> 00:34:59.280
- Yeah.
- I used to have

00:34:59.280 --> 00:35:02.943
a nice salary once upon a time.

00:35:03.840 --> 00:35:06.498
Not when I started in coaching,
but later in coaching.

00:35:06.498 --> 00:35:08.940
- Right.
- And so I do set my schedule,

00:35:08.940 --> 00:35:10.710
and it's wonderful, and it's coaching

00:35:10.710 --> 00:35:12.330
lots and lots of people.

00:35:12.330 --> 00:35:16.710
But if I wanted to go back
into coaching basketball,

00:35:16.710 --> 00:35:21.450
I don't think I could, because
it would take a visionary,

00:35:21.450 --> 00:35:23.970
it would take an athletic director

00:35:23.970 --> 00:35:26.850
that wasn't always
looking over his shoulder,

00:35:26.850 --> 00:35:28.383
which most of them do.

00:35:29.220 --> 00:35:31.771
And I love athletic directors,

00:35:31.771 --> 00:35:34.770
I've worked for many fabulous ones.

00:35:34.770 --> 00:35:39.130
But I have to say, it's
getting a little bit plastic

00:35:40.380 --> 00:35:43.110
in regard to Teflon,

00:35:43.110 --> 00:35:45.960
everything bounces off,
everything bounces off of people,

00:35:48.510 --> 00:35:50.853
everyone's actually operating from fear.

00:35:52.800 --> 00:35:55.350
And so I don't think I would be hired back

00:35:55.350 --> 00:35:57.853
even if I wanted that to be the case.

00:35:59.497 --> 00:36:03.440
- Yeah, and that's
really poignant as well.

00:36:03.440 --> 00:36:06.330
I mean, and I think to your
point, that fear is there,

00:36:06.330 --> 00:36:07.830
I'm assuming you're talking about even

00:36:07.830 --> 00:36:11.250
without thinking about
the mental health aspect

00:36:11.250 --> 00:36:13.230
And so you add.
- Yeah, I mean, I think.

00:36:13.230 --> 00:36:15.690
- later on in navigating, you know?

00:36:15.690 --> 00:36:20.200
- I just think that as a woman,
I am not a bipolar coach,

00:36:20.200 --> 00:36:21.990
I'm a coach with bipolar disorder.

00:36:21.990 --> 00:36:23.100
- Exactly, yeah.
- And that's

00:36:23.100 --> 00:36:24.750
